istance is three squares vertically. The white king can then move up to g6, then g7, etc. But the problem is that the white king might be too slow to catch up with the black king before the pawn promotes.\n\nAlternatively, if the white king heads towards the pawn. For example, moving to e5. Then from e5, next move to d5, etc. Let's think about the pawn's position. The pawn is on b3. The white king is at f5. Moving to e5, then d5, c5, b5. From there, the king can block the pawn. But again, this takes time.\n\nIn a king vs king and pawn endgame, the key is to reach the queening square in time or to block the pawn. Since the pawn is on b3, the queening square is b8. The black king is at g8. The black king is not near the pawn. So Black's king is far away from the pawn. So if White's king can get to the pawn's file in time, they can stop it. Otherwise, the pawn will promote.\n\nSo the plan for White is to march the king towards the pawn. So first move: from f5 to e5. Then d5, etc. Let's check if that's a valid move. Yes, K: f5-e5 is a valid move. Alternatively, moving to e6, then e5. But the immediate step is to go towards the pawn.\n\nAlternatively, moving to g5. Let's see. If White's king moves to g5, then the black king is at g8. The distance is three squares vertically. The black king can move towards the center, but White's king is now closer. However, this might not be as effective as moving towards the pawn.\n\nSo the best move is probably K: f5-e5. Moving towards the pawn. Then next move, e4, d4, etc. Wait, but the pawn is on b3. So moving left is better. From f5 to e5 is moving left one square. Then from e5 to d5, then c5, then b5. Once the white king is at b5, the pawn is on b3. Then the white king can follow as the pawn advances.\n\nAlternatively, maybe moving to e6 first. Let me think. From f5, moving to e6 would be towards the black king. But the black king is at g8. So maybe that's not helpful. The white king needs to get to the pawn's file. So left direct